You have two documented options, both built on Testcontainers [S1].

## 1. Service connections (`@ServiceConnection`)

Spring Boot can derive the connection details from the container itself, so you don't set a URL property. The `spring-boot-testcontainers` jar ships service connection factories that match container types. For example, `JdbcConnectionDetails` matches `JdbcDatabaseContainer`, and `R2dbcConnectionDetails` matches `PostgreSQLContainer`, `MySQLContainer`, `MariaDBContainer` and others. Mongo, Cassandra, Neo4j, Kafka, Redis-style services and more are covered too [S2].

The passages I found list the supported container types but don't include a `@ServiceConnection` code example. The annotation goes on the container field or bean. Check the Testcontainers page of the reference for the exact usage.

## 2. `@DynamicPropertySource`

This option is more verbose but more flexible [S6]. A static method registers properties whose values are read from the running container:

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() {
		// ...
	}

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}

}
```

The value is a supplier (`neo4j::getBoltUrl`). It's resolved after the container starts, so the random mapped port is picked up [S6].

## Slice tests

`@JdbcTest`, `@DataJdbcTest`, `@DataR2dbcTest` and `@DataJpaTest` use an in-memory embedded database by default. To run them against a real database such as your container, use `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase` [S3, S4, S5].

**Module:** `@DataJpaTest` comes from the `spring-boot-data-jpa-test` module [S7]. In Boot 4 the annotation lives in `org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest` [S3]. The search results don't give a starter or Maven/Gradle coordinates for it. They only name the module, so check the exact dependency declaration yourself.

**What it sets up by default:**
- It scans for `@Entity` classes and configures Spring Data JPA repositories [S7].
- It configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath [S7].
- It sets `spring.jpa.show-sql=true`, so SQL queries are logged. You can turn this off with the annotation's `showSql` attribute [S7].
- It makes a `TestEntityManager` available for injection. This is a test-oriented alternative to the standard `EntityManager` [S7].
- A `JdbcTemplate` is also available [S3].
- Tests are transactional and roll back after each test [S7].
- Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans are not scanned. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S7].

The docs say the full list of enabled auto-configurations is in the appendix. The search results don't include that appendix [S7].

**Common tweaks:**

Use a real database instead of the embedded one:
```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests { }
```
[S3]

Disable the transactional behaviour:
```java
@DataJpaTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyNonTransactionalTests { }
```
[S7]

You can add `TestEntityManager` to any Spring-based test with `@AutoConfigureTestEntityManager`. In that case, make sure the test runs in a transaction [S3]. Its package is `org.springframework.boot.jpa.test.autoconfigure.TestEntityManager` [S3].

Yes. Use `@DynamicPropertySource` with a Testcontainers container. A static method on the test class adds properties to the Spring `Environment`, and each value is supplied lazily through a `Supplier` [S1]:

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() {
		// ...
	}

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}

}
```

With this, the application's Neo4j beans connect to the container that Testcontainers manages [S1].

The docs describe this as a slightly more verbose but more flexible alternative to service connections [S1]. The passages I found don't cover service connections in detail.

For static values that don't come from a container, `TestPropertyValues` can add `key=value` properties to a `ConfigurableEnvironment` or `ConfigurableApplicationContext` [S2]. Unlike `@DynamicPropertySource`, it isn't tied to a container's lifecycle.

Annotate a field in your test class with `@MockitoBean`. Spring Framework provides this annotation, and it defines a Mockito mock for that bean in the `ApplicationContext` [S1]. You don't need a configuration class. Use `@MockitoSpyBean` if you want a Mockito spy rather than a mock [S1].

Here is the documentation's example, a `@WebFluxTest` slice test:

```java
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;

@WebFluxTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private WebTestClient webClient;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		this.webClient.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN).exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```
[S5]

The annotation comes from `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito`, not from a Spring Boot package [S5]. The Spring Boot docs I searched only briefly cover it and refer you to the Spring Framework documentation for details [S1].

Use `@MockitoSpyBean`. It's a Spring Framework annotation that Spring Boot's testing support points to for defining a Mockito spy on a bean in your `ApplicationContext` [S1]. It sits alongside `@MockitoBean`, which defines a Mockito mock [S1].

The Spring Boot reference only mentions it briefly and defers to the Spring Framework docs for details [S1]. It gives no `@MockitoSpyBean` code example, so the sketch below is not taken from the documentation I can search. It assumes the same package as the `@MockitoBean` import shown in the docs (`org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito`) [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @MockitoSpyBean
    private MyService myService;   // wraps the real bean in a Mockito spy

    @Test
    void callsService() {
        // exercise code that uses myService, then verify with Mockito
        // verify(myService).doSomething();
    }
}
```

For the exact semantics, such as stubbing, spying by type or name, and scoping, see the Spring Framework documentation on bean overrides. My search didn't return that material.

Use **`@WebMvcTest`**. It comes from the `spring-boot-webmvc-test` module. It loads only the Spring MVC slice, not the full application context, and it auto-configures `MockMvc` [S2].

**What gets scanned.** Only these bean types are picked up: `@Controller`, `@ControllerAdvice`, `@JacksonComponent`, `Converter`, `Filter`, `HandlerInterceptor`, `WebMvcConfigurer` and a few related types. Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans are not scanned [S2]. To include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans, use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` [S2].

**Collaborators.** The test is usually limited to one controller, and you supply its dependencies with `@MockitoBean` [S2].

**Assertions.** If AssertJ is available, `MockMvcTester` is auto-configured too [S2]. The documentation's example is:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;

@WebMvcTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN))
			.hasStatusOk()
			.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```

**Alternatives**
- `@SpringBootTest` with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` also gives you `MockMvc`. It loads the full context in a mock web environment without starting a server [S3, S4]. The docs suggest `@WebMvcTest` instead if you only want the web layer [S5].
- To use `RestTestClient` on the Mock MVC infrastructure, add `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` to your `@WebMvcTest` class [S2].

Use Spring REST Docs through `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. It comes from the `spring-boot-restdocs` module and replaces the Spring REST Docs JUnit extension [S2].

**Basic usage**

Add `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` to a `@WebMvcTest` class. It customizes the `MockMvc` bean to use REST Docs, so you inject it and use it as usual [S3]. The example below uses `MockMvcTester` and wraps the call in `document(...)`:

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
@AutoConfigureRestDocs
class MyUserDocumentationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@Test
	void listUsers() {
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/users").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN)).hasStatusOk()
			.apply(document("list-users"));
	}
}
```

`document` is statically imported from `MockMvcRestDocumentation`. `MockMvcTester` and `MockMvc` share the same underlying instance, so any configuration applies to both [S3].

**Output location and URIs**

Snippets go to `target/generated-snippets` with Maven or `build/generated-snippets` with Gradle. You can change this directory with `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. The same annotation sets the host, scheme and port that appear in documented URIs [S2].

**Document every call automatically**

Define a `RestDocumentationResultHandler` bean. The auto-configuration calls `alwaysDo` with it, so every `MockMvc` call generates the default snippets [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyResultHandlerConfiguration {

	@Bean
	public RestDocumentationResultHandler restDocumentation() {
		return MockMvcRestDocumentation.document("{method-name}");
	}
}
```

**Finer control**

Register a `RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer` bean when the annotation's attributes aren't enough. For example, this switches the snippets to Markdown [S3]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRestDocsConfiguration implements RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer {

	@Override
	public void customize(MockMvcRestDocumentationConfigurer configurer) {
		configurer.snippets().withTemplateFormat(TemplateFormats.markdown());
	}
}
```

For `WebTestClient`, the equivalent is a `WebTestClientBuilderCustomizer` bean that calls `entityExchangeResultConsumer(document("{method-name}"))` [S4].

Use `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)`. Each test run picks an available port at random. By default `@SpringBootTest` doesn't start a server and uses a mock environment instead [S2, S4].

Inject the port that was chosen with `@LocalServerPort`. To make REST calls, add `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and autowire a `RestTestClient`. It resolves relative URIs against the running server [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}

}
```

The imports are `org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureRestTestClient`, `org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment` and `org.springframework.test.web.servlet.client.RestTestClient` [S2].

Alternatives and notes from the docs:
- **AssertJ assertions:** wrap the response with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)` and use `assertThat(response).hasStatusOk()...` [S3].
- **WebFlux:** if `spring-webflux` is on the classpath, use `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` and autowire a `WebTestClient` [S3].
- **`TestRestTemplate`:** the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module also provides it [S3]. The passage I retrieved doesn't show its usage.
- **Management server:** `RANDOM_PORT` also starts it on a separate random port if your app configures a different port for it [S1].
- **Sliced tests:** you may need `@AutoConfigureWebServer` to get an embedded web server [S3].

The docs describe two helpers. The first is `TestRestTemplate`. The second, `RestTestClient`, is the newer option that the docs steer you toward when you want fluent assertions.

**`TestRestTemplate`**
- It's a convenience alternative to Spring's `RestTemplate` for integration tests. It comes from the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module, and you also need `spring-boot-restclient` [S1].
- Adding `spring-boot-restclient` enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses that builder, put the dependency on the main classpath and not only the test classpath [S1, S4].
- To inject it, use `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)` (or `DEFINED_PORT`) together with `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate`. URLs without a host and port go to the embedded server [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MyRandomPortTestRestTemplateTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate) {
		String body = restTemplate.getForObject("/", String.class);
		assertThat(body).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

**Security**
- You can get either a vanilla template or one that sends Basic HTTP authentication with a username and password [S1].

**Errors**
- The template is fault tolerant. It doesn't throw exceptions on 4xx and 5xx responses. You check the returned `ResponseEntity` and its status code instead [S1].

**Redirects**
- The passages I found don't describe redirect handling directly.
- The only related detail is that Apache HttpClient 5.1 or later is recommended. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures the client to suit [S1].

**Alternatives**
- `RestTestClient` is injected with `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient`. It resolves relative links to the running server and has a dedicated API for verifying responses [S5].
- `WebTestClient` is the equivalent for WebFlux [S1].

Use `@TestConfiguration`. It adds beans on top of your application's primary configuration, where a regular `@Configuration` would replace it [S3].

**Nested in the test class.** The nested class is picked up automatically and used in addition to the primary configuration [S3]. The docs describe this but the passages I found don't include a code example, so this one is my own sketch:

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @TestConfiguration
    static class Extra {
        @Bean
        Clock testClock() {
            return Clock.fixed(Instant.EPOCH, ZoneOffset.UTC);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void exampleTest() { /* ... */ }
}
```

**Top-level class, imported explicitly.** `@TestConfiguration` on a top-level class tells component scanning to skip it. This stops test-only configuration from being picked up by every test. Import it where you need it [S4]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestsConfiguration.class)
class MyTests {

    @Test
    void exampleTest() {
        // ...
    }

}
```

**Notes**
- A nested plain `@Configuration` class replaces your application's primary configuration. That is why `@TestConfiguration` is the right choice for additions [S3].
- An imported `@TestConfiguration` is processed before an inner-class one, and before any configuration found through component scanning. This only matters if you rely on bean overriding [S4].
- Spring caches application contexts between tests that share the same configuration, so the context loads only once for them [S3]. A test-specific configuration gives that test its own context, so use it only where you need it.
- If you use `@ComponentScan` directly rather than through `@SpringBootApplication`, you must register the `TypeExcludeFilter` on it. Otherwise top-level `@TestConfiguration` classes may still be scanned [S4].

**`@JdbcTest`**, from the `spring-boot-jdbc-test` module. It's meant for tests that only need a `DataSource` and don't use Spring Data JDBC [S1].

- By default it configures an in-memory embedded database and a `JdbcTemplate` [S1].
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` if you need specific `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S1].
- Tests are transactional and roll back after each test by default. To turn that off, add `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` [S1].
- To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase` [S1].

```java
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.JdbcTest;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@JdbcTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyTransactionalTests {

}
```

If you also need Spring Data JDBC repositories, use `@DataJdbcTest` instead [S2].

Use `@JsonTest`, from the `spring-boot-test-autoconfigure` module. It auto-configures only the JSON mapper, not the full application context [S1]. The mapper can be Jackson `JsonMapper` (including any `@JacksonComponent` beans and `JacksonModule`s), Gson, or Jsonb. The deprecated Jackson 2 `ObjectMapper` is also supported [S1].

It also gives you AssertJ-based tester helpers that work with JSONAssert and JsonPath. These are `JacksonTester`, `GsonTester`, `JsonbTester` and `BasicJsonTester` (for Strings). You can `@Autowire` them as fields on the test class [S1].

```java
@JsonTest
class MyJsonTests {

	@Autowired
	private JacksonTester<VehicleDetails> json;

	@Test
	void serialize() throws Exception {
		VehicleDetails details = new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic");
		// Assert against a `.json` file in the same package as the test
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).isEqualToJson("expected.json");
		// Or use JSON path based assertions
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).hasJsonPathStringValue("@.make");
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).extractingJsonPathStringValue("@.make").isEqualTo("Honda");
	}

	@Test
	void deserialize() throws Exception {
		String content = "{\"make\":\"Ford\",\"model\":\"Focus\"}";
		assertThat(this.json.parse(content)).isEqualTo(new VehicleDetails("Ford", "Focus"));
		assertThat(this.json.parseObject(content).getMake()).isEqualTo("Ford");
	}
}
```
[S3]

- **Plain unit tests:** you can use the helpers without `@JsonTest`. In that case, call the helper's `initFields` method in your `@BeforeEach` method [S3].
- **Configuration:** to adjust the auto-configuration, use `@AutoConfigureJsonTesters` [S1].
- **Number assertions:** `isEqualTo` may not work on a number at a JSON path, depending on its type. Use AssertJ's `satisfies` instead [S3]:

```java
assertThat(this.json.write(value)).extractingJsonPathNumberValue("@.test.numberValue")
	.satisfies((number) -> assertThat(number.floatValue()).isCloseTo(0.15f, within(0.01f)));
```
[S2]

Add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` to the test class [S6]:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests {

	// ...

}
```

By default, `@DataJpaTest` configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath [S8]. `Replace.NONE` stops that substitution, so the test uses the `DataSource` from your normal configuration. That means the usual `spring.datasource.*` properties, which you can override for tests. The docs I searched don't say how to start the real database, so that part is up to you.

`@DataJpaTest` tests are transactional and roll back after each test by default [S8]. You can turn that off with `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` [S8].

The same annotation works for `@DataJdbcTest`, `@JdbcTest` and `@DataR2dbcTest` [S2, S4, S7].

Use Spring Boot's `OutputCaptureExtension`. It's a JUnit extension that captures `System.out` and `System.err` [S1].

Add `@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)`. Then inject `CapturedOutput` into your test method, or into the test class constructor [S1].

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;

import org.springframework.boot.test.system.CapturedOutput;
import org.springframework.boot.test.system.OutputCaptureExtension;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)
class MyOutputCaptureTests {

	@Test
	void testName(CapturedOutput output) {
		System.out.println("Hello World!");
		assertThat(output).contains("World");
	}

}
```

The example in the docs passes `CapturedOutput` straight to AssertJ's `assertThat(...)` and checks it with `contains` [S1].

Yes. Declare each container as a `@Bean` in a test configuration class, and Spring Boot will manage it for you [S2].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {

	@Bean
	MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
		return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
	}

}
```

Import the configuration into any test that needs it, and `@Autowired` the container if you want to use it directly [S2]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestConfiguration.class)
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MongoDBContainer mongo;
	...
}
```

**Service connections.** This approach is often combined with `@ServiceConnection`, which wires the container's connection details into your app without manual properties [S2]. On a `@Bean` method, Spring Boot uses the method's return type to pick the connection details type. It doesn't call the method to read the image name. That works for typed containers like `Neo4jContainer` or `RabbitMQContainer`. For a `GenericContainer`, you have to supply a name hint [S6]:

```java
@Bean
@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
	return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7");
}
```

**Lifecycle benefits:**
- Container beans are created and started before all other beans. They are stopped after all other beans are destroyed [S3]. Beans that depend on the container can therefore clean up while it is still available.
- Each container is started once per application context in the TestContext Framework. A single instance is often reused across multiple test classes that share the cached context [S1].
- Containers are stopped when the context shuts down [S1].

If Testcontainers manages the containers instead, using its own annotations and extensions, the shutdown order isn't guaranteed. Containers can stop before the beans that use them, which can cause connection-loss exceptions [S1]. The docs also mention `@ImportTestcontainers` as another option for importing container declarations [S1]. The passages I found don't describe it in detail.

Use `@ServiceConnection` on a Testcontainers Redis container field in your `@DataRedisTest` class. The docs don't show a Redis-specific example, so the code below applies the documented Neo4j pattern [S5] to Redis. I haven't checked the Redis version of it against the docs.

```java
@DataRedisTest
@Testcontainers
class MyDataRedisTests {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static RedisContainer redis = new RedisContainer("redis:7");

    @Autowired
    private SomeRepository repository;

    // ...
}
```

How it works:

- **Annotations:** `@DataRedisTest` comes from the `spring-boot-data-redis-test` module [S2]. `@ServiceConnection` on the container field creates a connection details bean automatically [S5].
- **Connection details:** For Redis, that bean is `DataRedisConnectionDetails`. It's created for containers of type `RedisContainer` or `RedisStackContainer`, or for containers named "redis", "redis/redis-stack" or "redis/redis-stack-server" [S1].
- **Precedence:** The connection details take precedence over any connection-related configuration properties, so you don't need to set `spring.data.redis.host` or `spring.data.redis.port` [S5].
- **Dependency:** Add `spring-boot-testcontainers` as a test dependency [S5].
- **Static field:** Declare the container as a `static` field so Spring Boot can get the `Container` instance [S1].
- **Generic container:** If you use a `GenericContainer`, the image name must be "redis" for it to match [S1].
- **Imports:** The docs don't show the import for `RedisContainer`, so check that against the Testcontainers Redis module you use.

Use `@RestClientTest`. It comes from the `spring-boot-restclient-test` module, in the package `org.springframework.boot.restclient.test.autoconfigure` [S2].

By default it does the following [S2]:
- It auto-configures Jackson, GSON, and Jsonb support.
- It configures a `RestTemplateBuilder` and a `RestClient.Builder`.
- It adds support for `MockRestServiceServer`, which you can `@Autowire`.

Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans aren't scanned. Name the beans you want to test with the `value` or `components` attribute. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` if you need `@ConfigurationProperties` beans [S2].

```java
@RestClientTest(RemoteVehicleDetailsService.class)
class MyRestClientServiceTests {

	@Autowired
	private RemoteVehicleDetailsService service;

	@Autowired
	private MockRestServiceServer server;

	@Test
	void getVehicleDetailsWhenResultIsSuccessShouldReturnDetails() {
		this.server.expect(requestTo("https://example.com/greet/details"))
			.andRespond(withSuccess("hello", MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN));
		String greeting = this.service.callRestService();
		assertThat(greeting).isEqualTo("hello");
	}
}
```
[S1]

**Base URI in expectations:**
- If your code uses a `RestClient.Builder`, or a `RestTemplateBuilder` without `baseUri(...)`, use the full URI in the expectations, as above [S1].
- If your code calls `RestTemplateBuilder.baseUri(...)`, leave the base URI out. For example, use `requestTo("/greet/details")` [S2].
